cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or reference this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Sepiola: A WebMCP Telestrator — the page is the screen, not the actor"
abstract: >
  WebMCP lets a web page register tools an in-browser agent can call; nearly every
  example makes the page the actor. Sepiola demonstrates the complementary pattern,
  the telestrator: a page that exposes tools which show rather than do, so an agent
  whose intelligence lives elsewhere can draw it onto a persistent surface the human
  is also looking at. Three roles are kept apart — an analyst (the CHIRP MCP server)
  that decides, a pen (any WebMCP agent) that draws, and a screen (this page) that
  has no opinions, enforced by a test in which every rendered text node must come
  from the analyst's read or the interface copy. The seven broadcast moves are
  registered against Chromium's real WebMCP implementation; the analyst is hosted
  on Cloudflare Workers and the screen on Cloudflare Pages.
